From: Gran Turismo 6 for Mac <https://github.com/Wooloomooloo2/Gran-Turismo-6-Mac>
Subject: [PATCH] cellFs: mkdir, rename, truncate and write-opens only on writable mounts

Upstream: not yet submitted; written to be upstreamed (generic, no title names)
Licence: MIT, as ps3recomp (see its LICENSE); offered under the same terms
Base: ps3recomp 4e8f3ca87a3e1d87bb83b8b7e9348fe2685cd514 (after 0001-0023)

What: patch 0020 confined cellFsUnlink and cellFsRmdir to /dev_hdd0 and
/dev_hdd1 by an allowlist. The other calls that change the file system did
not have it:
- cellFsMkdir went through host_path(), which resolves an unknown device, a
  relative path or '..' into the VFS root (the disc image), created every
  missing parent and answered CELL_OK whatever happened;
- cellFsRename and cellFsTruncate were libs/filesystem/cellFs.c's, whose
  prefix map sends /dev_bdvd to the disc image, has no '..' handling, and
  (rename) created the target's missing parents and replaced an existing one;
- cellFsOpen honoured O_WRONLY/O_RDWR, O_CREAT and O_TRUNC on any path.
runtime/ppu/ppu_fs.cpp now resolves all of them through one function,
fs_target_of() (0020's canonical path, allowlist and realpath() parent check,
now shared), registers cellFsRename (0xF12EECC8) and cellFsTruncate
(0xC9DC3AC5) itself, and answers as lv2 does under RPCS3 (behaviour only; no
RPCS3 code):
- mkdir: one level; EEXIST when it exists (also "/" and a mount itself),
  ENOENT for a missing parent, ENOTDIR under a file, EROFS on a read-only
  mount;
- rename: EPERM for "/", ENOTMOUNTED, EXDEV across devices, EROFS on a
  read-only mount, EEXIST for an existing target (lv2 does not replace it;
  renamex_np(RENAME_EXCL) on macOS), ENOENT for a missing source or target
  folder; a mount itself is EBUSY;
- truncate: EISDIR for "/" and a directory, EROFS, ENOENT;
- open with write access, O_CREAT or O_TRUNC: on /dev_hdd0 and /dev_hdd1 the
  canonical path with no fallback into another mount; on a read-only mount
  EPERM for write access to or O_TRUNC of an existing file and for O_CREAT of
  a missing one, otherwise the file opens read-only; "/" EISDIR; O_EXCL is
  now honoured (EEXIST); an open failure answers EEXIST/ENOTDIR/EISDIR when
  the host says so (it was always ENOENT);
- everywhere: a relative path is ENOENT and an unknown device ENOTMOUNTED, a
  symlink out of a writable mount answers as a read-only mount would, a
  trailing '/' after a file is ENOTDIR (it removed the file), a host path that
  does not fit is ENAMETOOLONG, and realpath() gets PATH_MAX buffers.
unlink and rmdir keep 0020's answers, except "/" (EISDIR / EPERM, as lv2,
where it was ENOTMOUNTED). Read-only opens and stat are unchanged.

Why: a title's first boot made folders inside the disc image (a relative
mkdir), and a rename or an O_TRUNC open through the same paths could replace,
move or empty a disc file.

Test: runtime/ppu/tests/test_fs_imports.cpp (run by
tools/test_ppu_fs_imports.py) gains a write-guard block on scratch folders:
every answer above, with checks that the disc folder and a folder outside the
mounts are unchanged, plus trailing-slash, wrong-case (/DEV_HDD0) and long
path cases for unlink.

